<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Jersey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New Jersey is a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> between the Hudson and Delaware rivers. Taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1664, it was granted to Lord Berkeley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-carteret">Sir George Carteret</a> and divided into East and West Jersey before being reunited as a royal province in 1702. In 1776 it adopted a state constitution and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-jersey.jpg" alt="New Jersey" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: author of xrmap, with modifications by Denelson83 and ChrisRuvolo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Jersey.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>New Jersey</b> is named after the island: in 1664 the Duke of York granted the land to Sir George Carteret, a Jerseyman who had held the island for the Stuarts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In one American state women voted for a time: the <b>New Jersey</b> constitution of 1776 gave the vote to inhabitants with enough property, and propertied single women and widows voted there until a law of 1807 confined the franchise to free white men.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Samuel A. Ward, organist and choirmaster of Grace Episcopal Church in Newark, <b>New Jersey</b>, had written a hymn tune in 1882; it came to him on a ferry from Coney Island back to his home after a summer day, and he wrote it down at once.</blockquote><a href="/songs/america-the-beautiful">America the Beautiful</a></li><li><blockquote>By Nelson's account the real John Henry was born in 1848 in <b>New Jersey</b> and died of silicosis, the lung disease caused by the dust of drilling and blasting, rather than of exhaustion, and he stands for the many hundreds of convict labourers who died in the mountains…</blockquote><a href="/songs/john-henry">John Henry</a></li><li><blockquote>Soldiers of the Pennsylvania Line under Anthony Wayne rose in mutiny at the Continental Army's winter camp in Morristown, <b>New Jersey</b>, demanding higher pay and better housing.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Hughes flew his H-1 racer from Burbank, California, to Newark, <b>New Jersey</b>, in 7 hours, 28 minutes and 25 seconds.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
